{
  "nodes": [
    {
      "id": "central",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 950,
      "y": 1050,
      "width": 400,
      "height": 260,
      "text": "# 🖥️ CPU\n**Central Processing Unit**\n\nThe \"brain\" of the computer. Executes program instructions by performing arithmetic, logical, control, and input/output (I/O) operations.\n\nAlso called the **Microprocessor** when fabricated on a single IC chip.",
      "color": "1"
    },
    {
      "id": "g1",
      "type": "group",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 650,
      "label": "🔧 Core Components",
      "color": "2"
    },
    {
      "id": "alu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 80,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "2",
      "text": "**ALU**\n(Arithmetic Logic Unit)\n\nPerforms all arithmetic (+, −, ×, ÷) and logical (AND, OR, NOT, comparisons) operations."
    },
    {
      "id": "cu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 460,
      "y": 80,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "2",
      "text": "**CU**\n(Control Unit)\n\nDirects/coordinates all CPU activity — fetches, decodes & directs execution of instructions.\n\n⚠️ Exam trap: CU does **NOT** itself process data, it only controls."
    },
    {
      "id": "registers",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 340,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 260,
      "color": "2",
      "text": "**Registers**\n\nSmall, very high-speed storage locations inside the CPU.\n\nKey registers:\n• Accumulator (AC)\n• Program Counter (PC)\n• Instruction Register (IR)\n• Memory Address Register (MAR)\n• Memory Buffer/Data Register (MBR/MDR)"
    },
    {
      "id": "cache",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 460,
      "y": 340,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 260,
      "color": "2",
      "text": "**Cache Memory**\n\nUltra-fast memory sitting between CPU and RAM; stores frequently used data/instructions.\n\nLevels (fastest→slowest, smallest→largest):\nL1 → L2 → L3\n\n⚠️ Cache ≠ RAM (cache is smaller & faster, closer to CPU)."
    },
    {
      "id": "g2",
      "type": "group",
      "x": 1000,
      "y": 0,
      "width": 1700,
      "height": 340,
      "label": "🔄 Fetch–Decode–Execute Cycle (Machine Cycle)",
      "color": "5"
    },
    {
      "id": "fetch",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 90,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "5",
      "text": "**1. Fetch**\n\nCU retrieves the next instruction from memory (address held in the Program Counter) and loads it into the Instruction Register."
    },
    {
      "id": "decode",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1460,
      "y": 90,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "5",
      "text": "**2. Decode**\n\nCU interprets the fetched instruction to determine what operation is required and which data/registers are involved."
    },
    {
      "id": "execute",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1880,
      "y": 90,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "5",
      "text": "**3. Execute**\n\nALU (or other unit) actually carries out the instruction — a calculation, data move, comparison, etc."
    },
    {
      "id": "store",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2300,
      "y": 90,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "5",
      "text": "**4. Store**\n\nResult of execution is written back to a register or memory. PC updates to point to the next instruction."
    },
    {
      "id": "g3",
      "type": "group",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 750,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 650,
      "label": "🏗️ Architecture Types",
      "color": "6"
    },
    {
      "id": "vonneumann",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 830,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "6",
      "text": "**Von Neumann Architecture**\n\nSingle shared memory & bus for both data AND instructions. Most common CPU design.\n\nLimitation: \"Von Neumann bottleneck\" — can't fetch data & instructions at the same time."
    },
    {
      "id": "harvard",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 460,
      "y": 830,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "6",
      "text": "**Harvard Architecture**\n\nSeparate memory & buses for data and instructions → can access both simultaneously (faster).\n\nUsed in many microcontrollers/DSPs."
    },
    {
      "id": "risc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 1090,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 260,
      "color": "6",
      "text": "**RISC**\nReduced Instruction Set Computer\n\nSmall set of simple, fast instructions. Power-efficient.\n\nExample: **ARM** — used in most smartphones."
    },
    {
      "id": "cisc",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 460,
      "y": 1090,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 260,
      "color": "6",
      "text": "**CISC**\nComplex Instruction Set Computer\n\nLarge set of complex instructions; does more work per instruction.\n\nExample: **Intel/AMD x86** processors."
    },
    {
      "id": "g4",
      "type": "group",
      "x": 1000,
      "y": 400,
      "width": 1700,
      "height": 550,
      "label": "📊 Specifications & Performance",
      "color": "4"
    },
    {
      "id": "clockspeed",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 480,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 200,
      "color": "4",
      "text": "**Clock Speed**\n(Clock Rate)\n\nMeasured in GHz (billion cycles/sec). Higher = more instruction cycles per second.\n\n⚠️ Not the only factor in real-world performance."
    },
    {
      "id": "cores",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1460,
      "y": 480,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 200,
      "color": "4",
      "text": "**Cores**\n\nIndependent processing units on one chip.\nDual-core = 2, Quad-core = 4, Octa-core = 8.\n\nMore cores → better multitasking/parallel processing."
    },
    {
      "id": "wordlength",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1880,
      "y": 480,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 200,
      "color": "4",
      "text": "**Word Length**\n\nNumber of bits the CPU processes in one operation (e.g., 32-bit, 64-bit).\n\nDetermines data handled per cycle & max addressable memory."
    },
    {
      "id": "perfunits",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1040,
      "y": 720,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 200,
      "color": "4",
      "text": "**Performance Units**\n\n• MIPS — Million Instructions Per Second\n• FLOPS — Floating Point Operations Per Second"
    },
    {
      "id": "hyperthreading",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 1460,
      "y": 720,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 200,
      "color": "4",
      "text": "**Hyper-Threading / SMT**\n\nLets one physical core act as two logical cores, improving multitasking efficiency."
    },
    {
      "id": "g5",
      "type": "group",
      "x": 2050,
      "y": 1150,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 650,
      "label": "🏭 Manufacturers & Examples",
      "color": "3"
    },
    {
      "id": "intel",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2090,
      "y": 1230,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "3",
      "text": "**Intel**\n\nFounded 1968. Lines: Pentium, Celeron, Core i3/i5/i7/i9.\n\nMade the **4004** (1971) — world's first commercial microprocessor."
    },
    {
      "id": "amd",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2510,
      "y": 1230,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 220,
      "color": "3",
      "text": "**AMD**\nAdvanced Micro Devices\n\nMajor Intel competitor. Known for Ryzen, Athlon, EPYC series."
    },
    {
      "id": "arm",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2090,
      "y": 1490,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 260,
      "color": "3",
      "text": "**ARM**\n\nDesigns RISC-based CPU architecture, licensed to many manufacturers.\n\nPowers most smartphones & tablets — known for low power use."
    },
    {
      "id": "apple",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 2510,
      "y": 1490,
      "width": 380,
      "height": 260,
      "color": "3",
      "text": "**Apple Silicon**\n\nARM-based chips (M1, M2, M3…) designed in-house by Apple, replacing Intel chips in Macs since 2020."
    },
    {
      "id": "g6",
      "type": "group",
      "x": 0,
      "y": 1450,
      "width": 1900,
      "height": 400,
      "label": "⭐ LDC Exam Quick Facts",
      "color": "1"
    },
    {
      "id": "quickfacts",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 40,
      "y": 1530,
      "width": 900,
      "height": 300,
      "color": "1",
      "text": "**Quick Facts**\n\n• CPU = \"Brain of the Computer\"\n• First microprocessor: **Intel 4004 (1971)**\n• Speed measured in Hertz (Hz)/GHz\n• 3 main parts: ALU + CU + Registers\n• **Moore's Law**: transistor count doubles ~every 2 years\n• Motherboard houses & connects the CPU\n• Socket types: LGA, PGA, BGA"
    },
    {
      "id": "mcqtraps",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 980,
      "y": 1530,
      "width": 880,
      "height": 300,
      "color": "1",
      "text": "**Common MCQ Traps**\n\n• CU does NOT process data — only controls/directs\n• RAM ≠ Cache (cache is faster, smaller, closer to CPU)\n• Microprocessor = CPU on a single IC chip\n• Clock speed alone ≠ performance (cores, cache, architecture also matter)"
    }
  ],
